{"name":"Carlisle","hex":"#45867c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Carlisle","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in and the county town of Cumbria, and formerly of the county of Cumberland, and in Cumberland unitary authority since 2023, northwestern England .","A former local government district in Cumbria, existing in and around the city, created 1974 and abolished 2023, becoming part of Cumberland unitary authority."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Carlisle"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carlisle","blocks":[{"text":"Carlisle is a city located in the Cumberland district of Cumbria, England. It is the largest settlement and only city in Cumbria. In 2021, it had a population of 77,730."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Carlisle_Market_Cross_6310907_2233337d.jpg/330px-Carlisle_Market_Cross_6310907_2233337d.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlisle","wikidata":"Q192896"},{"title":"Carlisle (district, 1974–2023)","blocks":[{"text":"The City of Carlisle was a local government district of Cumbria, England, with the status of a city. It was named after its largest settlement, Carlisle, but covered a far larger area which included the towns of Brampton and Longtown, as well as outlying villages including Dalston, Scotby and Wetheral. In 2011 the district had a population of 107,524, and an area of 1,039.97 square kilometres (402 sq mi)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Carlisle_Council_Offices.jpg/330px-Carlisle_Council_Offices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlisle_(district%2C_1974%E2%80%932023)","wikidata":"Q1094110"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/1208430_Cathedral_Church_of_the_Holy_and_Undivided_Trinity%2C_seen_from_the_Southeast_Carlisle